1
00:00:00,497 --> 00:00:03,115
An invoice can pass every arithmetic check

2
00:00:03,155 --> 00:00:05,081
and still contain the wrong amounts.

3
00:00:05,182 --> 00:00:06,100
Let me show you.

4
00:00:06,213 --> 00:00:07,884
I upload a dollar invoice.

5
00:00:07,985 --> 00:00:12,593
MiniCPM-OCR reads its payment region into editable fields,

6
00:00:12,633 --> 00:00:14,256
keeping the source beside them.

7
00:00:14,356 --> 00:00:17,326
Now I deliberately multiply every amount by ten.

8
00:00:17,427 --> 00:00:20,032
Subtotal, shipping and tax still add up.

9
00:00:20,145 --> 00:00:21,355
The rules pass,

10
00:00:21,407 --> 00:00:24,013
although the original total is clearly different.

11
00:00:24,114 --> 00:00:26,622
OCR Jev checks this against the page.

12
00:00:26,723 --> 00:00:29,656
It receives the source image, the candidate text,

13
00:00:29,696 --> 00:00:31,780
and an image rendered from that text.

14
00:00:31,881 --> 00:00:34,280
That rendered copy makes the claimed content

15
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:35,882
visible for comparison.

16
00:00:35,983 --> 00:00:39,923
Inside the model, the inputs become a shared representation.

17
00:00:40,024 --> 00:00:42,848
A small classification head scores two outcomes:

18
00:00:42,888 --> 00:00:44,875
consistent and inconsistent.

19
00:00:44,975 --> 00:00:47,363
It returns a consistency score directly.

20
00:00:47,463 --> 00:00:50,299
Higher scores mean stronger agreement with the source.

21
00:00:50,412 --> 00:00:53,916
The correct candidate scores nearly 0.99.

22
00:00:54,016 --> 00:00:57,969
Our altered candidate scores just 0.004.

23
00:00:58,070 --> 00:00:59,826
We compare that score with a threshold

24
00:00:59,866 --> 00:01:02,545
chosen on separate calibration examples.

25
00:01:02,645 --> 00:01:04,802
This invoice enters the review queue.

26
00:01:04,902 --> 00:01:06,683
The reviewer opens its source,

27
00:01:06,723 --> 00:01:08,358
restores the correct amounts,

28
00:01:08,398 --> 00:01:09,935
and confirms the correction.

29
00:01:10,084 --> 00:01:12,799
For accounts payable, the corrected fields can continue

30
00:01:12,839 --> 00:01:15,372
to purchase order matching and approval,

31
00:01:15,412 --> 00:01:16,914
with their evidence attached.

32
00:01:17,026 --> 00:01:20,045
I also tested whether this extra model actually helps.

33
00:01:20,145 --> 00:01:22,350
On eighty held out synthetic invoices,

34
00:01:22,390 --> 00:01:24,851
rules caught all three natural errors.

35
00:01:24,963 --> 00:01:27,059
The verifier added thirteen reviews

36
00:01:27,099 --> 00:01:28,843
and caught no extra errors.

37
00:01:28,944 --> 00:01:30,421
In a separate stress test,

38
00:01:30,461 --> 00:01:33,576
it flagged all twenty four deliberately altered candidates

39
00:01:33,616 --> 00:01:34,948
that passed the rules.

40
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:38,516
The useful measure is errors caught per unit of review work.

41
00:01:38,616 --> 00:01:41,100
The online lab lets you inspect every case

42
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:43,101
and explore that tradeoff yourself.
